<h2>The dispute in outline</h2>
<p>The landlady sought eviction of the tenants — successors of the original tenant — from a shop in the Nai Sarak commercial stretch, pleading that she required it to run a tailoring business of her own. The petition travelled the special track that Section 25B of the Delhi Rent Control Act lays down for bona fide requirement cases: summons in the statutory form, an affidavit seeking leave to defend, and a decision on whether any triable issue exists before the case can go to trial at all. By order dated 13 May 2024, the Additional Rent Controller (Central) dismissed the leave-to-defend application and passed an eviction order. The tenants invoked the High Court&rsquo;s revisional jurisdiction under the proviso to Section 25B(8).</p>

<h2>Three defences, three answers</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>The withdrawn 2008 petition</h3><p>An earlier eviction case, filed for the landlady and her son and withdrawn in 2019, was said to show the need had been met. The court disagreed: the withdrawal decided nothing about her requirement, and the fresh petition stood on its own feet.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The family&rsquo;s other shops</h3><p>Adjoining shops run by the son&rsquo;s musical-instruments business, premises in storage or disrepair, properties sold years before, upper floors, and a shop in a sitting tenant&rsquo;s occupation — none amounted to alternative accommodation that was both suitable and available to her.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Tailoring from home</h3><p>That the landlady stitched from her residence proved the vocation, not the absence of need. A home operation is no substitute for a ground-floor shop on a commercial street, and the law does not force a landlord to make do.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The crucial date</h3><p>Need is tested at the filing of the petition; later events count only if they overwhelm the requirement entirely. Family use of neighbouring premises after filing did not eclipse the landlady&rsquo;s own claim to this shop.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Why the revision could not become an appeal</h2>
<p>The judgment restates the boundary that decides most Section 25B(8) proceedings before it ever reaches the facts: the High Court&rsquo;s jurisdiction is supervisory. Following the Supreme Court&rsquo;s exposition in <em>Abid-Ul-Islam v. Inder Sain Dua</em>, the court examines the legality of the process — whether the Controller applied the right tests, considered the material, and reached a conclusion the record can bear — and stops there. Re-appreciation of evidence, the staple of a first appeal, is outside the remit. The tenants&rsquo; arguments, pressed as triable issues, were in substance invitations to re-weigh what the Controller had already weighed: property-tax receipts against possession realities, the geography of the family&rsquo;s shops, the credibility of the stated need. The court worked through each and found the Controller&rsquo;s reasoning sound, which ended the matter.</p>

<h2>The leave-to-defend threshold, applied</h2>
<p>Section 25B&rsquo;s architecture is deliberately asymmetric. The landlord&rsquo;s bona fide requirement petition triggers a presumption-friendly summary procedure; the tenant&rsquo;s affidavit must disclose facts that, if proved, would defeat the claim — a genuine dispute about ownership, the reality of the need, or the existence of suitable alternative accommodation. The judgment illustrates how particularised that disclosure must be. Pleading that the landlord&rsquo;s family holds other premises is not enough; the tenant must show those premises are actually available to the petitioning landlord and reasonably suitable for the stated need. Pleading that the landlord already earns is not enough; earning elsewhere does not negate the need to earn from one&rsquo;s own property in one&rsquo;s own way. The courts&rsquo; consistent refusal to dictate the landlord&rsquo;s business choices — which trade, which premises, which floor — leaves tenants a narrow target, and affidavits that miss it produce eviction orders without trial.</p>

<h2>Takeaways for landlords and tenants</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>For landlords:</strong> plead the need with specifics — the intended business, why this premises suits it, and a candid account of other holdings with reasons each is unavailable or unsuitable. Candour at the pleading stage pre-empts the leave-to-defend affidavit.</li>
  <li><strong>For tenants:</strong> investigate before affirming. An affidavit listing the landlord&rsquo;s family properties without proving availability and suitability invites outright refusal of leave; municipal records, site photographs and possession particulars are the minimum.</li>
  <li><strong>For both:</strong> remember the timeline discipline — the requirement is judged at filing, and the revision tests process, not merits. The real battle in a Section 14(1)(e) case is fought in the first affidavits, not in the High Court.</li>
  <li><strong>After eviction:</strong> Section 14(7)&rsquo;s statutory breathing period, where applicable, governs when the landlord may obtain possession — the judgment expressly left execution subject to it.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Successors of long-deceased tenants hold rent-controlled commercial premises across old Delhi&rsquo;s markets, and bona fide requirement petitions remain the principal route by which owners recover them. Both sides should treat the leave-to-defend stage as the trial it effectively is: the tenant&rsquo;s affidavit is the one chance to raise a triable issue, and the landlord&rsquo;s reply is the place to demolish it with documents rather than adjectives.</p>
</div>
